    reg {
        name = "Channel frame-work words (VPSS_HAL_SetChnFrameWork), part 1";
        desc = "Node image offset 0x02C. VPSS_HAL_SetChnFrameWork writes six words in four jump-table cases: 0x2C and 0x38 each hold two 4x4-bit groups (a channel pair uses [31:16], the other [15:0]), 0x30/0x34/0x3C/0x40 hold three bytes at [7:0], [15:8], [23:16]. 'Frame work' is the vendor name of a per-channel structure (probably the output frame geometry/format handling: the byte triples look like per-plane or per-field values); the semantic was UNKNOWN. Vendor pass 2026-11-11 (VpssCalcFramework, VPSS_DRV_CheckChnFrameWork): 'frame work' is a per-channel BORDER drawn around the picture: the four nibbles are four border widths (each must be even and <= 14, checked by VPSS_DRV_CheckChnFrameWork, error 'frame width'), the byte triple is the border color in Y [7:0], Cb [15:8], Cr [23:16] (software converts a 24-bit RGB color with the BT.601 integer formula Y=((66R+129G+25B)>>8)+16, Cb=((-38R-74G+112B)>>8)+128, Cr=((112R-94G-18B)>>8)+128; all four colors of a channel must be equal). Channel to word map (HAL jump table): chn0 = 0x2C[15:0] + color 0x30, chn1 = 0x2C[31:16] + color 0x34, chn2 = 0x38[15:0] + color 0x3C, chn3 = 0x38[31:16] + color 0x40. Confidence: word/bit layout HIGH, 'border' meaning MEDIUM (inferred from the check function and the RGB conversion; which nibble is top/bottom/left/right is not known).  Source: hi3520D_vpss.ko. Not verified on hardware.";
